# sys_mmap.tkr — the §16 fifth-crumb fixture (arena-over-mmap keystone sub-crumb A). `main.tks`
# issues `teko::sys::syscall6(sys::SYS_MMAP, 0, 4096, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
# MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0)` — the raw x86_64 Linux `mmap` syscall requesting one
# anonymous private page — and checks the returned address is NOT in the `[-4095, -1]` errno
# band before issuing `teko::sys::syscall2(sys::SYS_MUNMAP, addr, 4096)` to release it and
# checking that call returned `0`. The harness observes exit code 0, proving the `syscall6`/
# `syscall2` intrinsics issued the real mmap/munmap instructions and the kernel honoured both.

Feature: sys_mmap — the §16 syscall6/syscall2 intrinsics issue x86_64 Linux mmap/munmap of one anonymous page

  Scenario: raw_mmap_then_munmap_succeeds_and_exits_zero — the process maps then unmaps one page and exits 0
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
